== quoted forms: -5 is a unary-op call, not a literal (pattern and expr alike)
-5   =>   {:-, [line: 1], [5]}
case x do -5 -> 1 end   =>   {:case, [line: 1], [{:x, [line: 1], nil}, [do: [{:->, [line: 1], [[{:-, [line: 1], [5]}], 1]}]]]}
x when x >= -5   =>   {:when, [line: 1], [{:x, [line: 1], nil}, {:>=, [line: 1], [{:x, [line: 1], nil}, {:-, [line: 1], [5]}]}]}
-5..5   =>   {:.., [line: 1], [{:-, [line: 1], [5]}, 5]}
2+3   =>   {:+, [line: 1], [2, 3]}
@spec f(-5..5) :: :ok   =>   {:@, [line: 1], [{:spec, [line: 1], [{:"::", [line: 1], [{:f, [line: 1], [{:.., [line: 1], [{:-, [line: 1], [5]}, 5]}]}, :ok]}]}]}
== Does -5 work in a pattern, a guard, a range pattern, an expression-folded pattern?
{:m5, :lt, :in, :out}
